#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124170 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0015e19e-222b-5c5b-a020-93392c50c10e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Best Linear Approximation of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "37734955-23b7-52d1-881d-0c77f672c35a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3162" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y, z)= xyz+y^2$. Find the best linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$." :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b, z-c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, -1, 1)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(3,-1, 1) + ∇f(3,-1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3, -1, 1)= -2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}f_{x} &= yz & ⇒ & f_{x}(3,-1, 1) =-1, \\\\ f_{y}|= xz + 2y & ⇒ & f_{y}(3,-1, 1) = 1 \\\\ f_{z}|= xy & ⇒ & f_{z}(3,-1, 1) =-3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(3, -1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(3,-1) &= ⟨ - 1, 1,-3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(3,-1, 1) + ∇f(3,-1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩; =-2 + ⟨ - 1, 1,-3⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 3, y + 1, z - 1⟩; =-2 - (x - 3) + (y + 1) - 3(z - 1); =-2 - x + 3 + y + 1 - 3z + 3; = 5 - x + y - 3z\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "839eb8fd-39b8-5f1f-b833-7134d39cdf2d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(1,1) = 1, \\quad f_x(x,y) = 2xy, \\quad f_y(x,y) = x^2$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$, estimate the value of $f(1.1, 0.9)$." :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y) \\mid \\approx f(1, 1) + ∇f(1, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩; = f(1, 1) + ⟨f_{x}(1, 1), f_{y}(1, 1)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩; = 1 + ⟨f_{x}(1, 1), f_{y}(1, 1)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 1⟩\n$$\n\nNow, we find $f_x(1,1)$ and $f_y(1,1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{x}(1, 1) &= 2(1)(1) = 2 \\\\\nf_{y}(1, 1) &= (1)^{2} = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx 1 + \\langle 2, 1 \\rangle \\cdot \\langle x-1, y-1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.1, 0.9)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.1$ and $y=0.9$, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(1.1, 0.9) \\mid \\approx 1 + ⟨2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨1.1 - 1, 0.9 - 1⟩; = 1 + ⟨2, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0.1,-0.1⟩; = 1 + 0.2 - 0.1; = 1.1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/id #uuid "54ac5be6-5b6a-58a3-a960-0d9f18e7fc88" :question/math-academy-id "e-8692" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y, z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(1,2,5)=2, \\quad f_x(1,2,5)=4, \\quad f_y(1,2,5)=1, \\quad f_z(1,2,5)=-2$.\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$." :question/worked-solution "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b, c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,5)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x, y, z) \\mid \\approx f(1, 2, 5) + ∇f(1, 2, 5) \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩; = f(1, 2, 5) + ⟨f_{x}(1, 2, 5), f_{y}(1, 2, 5), f_{z}(1, 2, 5)⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩; = 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 1, y - 2, z - 5⟩\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,2.02,4.95)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x= 1.01$, $y=2.02$, and $z=4.95$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(1.01, 2.02, 4.95) \\mid \\approx 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨1.01 - 1, 2.02 - 2, 4.95 - 5⟩; = 2 + ⟨4, 1,-2⟩ \\cdot ⟨0.01, 0.02,-0.05⟩; = 2 + 0.04 + 0.02 + 0.1; = 2.16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "27e3c802-d356-5a2f-bdda-218144956722" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Two-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b06e6a24-212e-526f-9147-008af64dc863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124043} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4cc936-bbf5-5d4e-82ff-3362dfb93420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124044} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3c63b8-336a-5573-9934-bf5eeffccaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124045} {:answer/id #uuid "266cdc7d-e4b2-5880-bdca-ae039b83be26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124046} {:answer/id #uuid "2acee06a-76bf-508c-b806-84e79ab5c5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a3c63b8-336a-5573-9934-bf5eeffccaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124045} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6d5649-241f-520d-812f-da76cefb5e40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124042}] :question/id #uuid "afa045a3-8af9-5fbd-84c3-80bfb9bc8dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97876" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nSuppose that $f(x, y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n$f(2, 5) = 6, f_{x}(2, 5) = 1, f_{y}(2, 5) =-1$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2, 5)$, estimate the value of $f(2.06, 4.92)$" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1639105-dca4-50d1-8d86-a7aed03ba0b5" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Value of a Three-Variable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf47458-d1bb-5e5d-a1ef-8ecf14cec76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.825" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124061} {:answer/id #uuid "45df04c7-24aa-528a-b019-939c01335f77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124062} {:answer/id #uuid "52a8f962-89bf-5ac5-bd5c-b7cb41305c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124063} {:answer/id #uuid "63d0eaf0-ef34-5a25-91d0-5fcaa4c2bac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.070" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124064} {:answer/id #uuid "70be2157-e12a-545f-b89d-0b1ea28139fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.905" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a8f962-89bf-5ac5-bd5c-b7cb41305c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.985" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124063} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa380345-c9ac-541e-9eb0-e9e89ed5fef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124060}] :question/id #uuid "f9b9dce4-8962-515d-a80e-a7650e02e517" :question/math-academy-id "q-97879" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f(x, y, z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n$f(3, 3, 1) = 3, f_{x}(x, y, z) = \\frac{yz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}, f_{y}(x, y, z) = \\frac{xz}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}, f_{z}(x, y, z) = \\frac{xy}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}$.\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3, 3, 1)$, estimate the value of $f(3.01, 3.02, 0.98)$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
